00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this morning we are tracking the very  close vote count in the presidential  election  we are also looking at the president's  claims claims without any any evidence  anyone has seen so far  that democrats are in his words quote  trying to rig  an election here is part of what the  president said last night  at the white house we think we will win  the election very easily we think  there's going to be a lot of litigation  because we have so much evidence so much  proof and  it's going to end up perhaps at the  highest court in the land  we'll see again no one has seen that  evidence no it has seen that proof  joining us now  is cbs news contributor david becker he  is an election law expert and the  founder of the center for election  innovation and research  and perhaps one of the only election  experts in america not currently working  for one of the two campaigns  david very glad to have you with us this  morning  i want to go into some of the state by  state claims from the white house but i  want to begin big picture  the president says if every quote legal  vote is counted he'd win we just also  heard mitch mcconnell  uh say that legal votes should be  counted what is the legal definition of  a quote legal vote  well it varies by state but one thing we  know for sure is that all of the ballots  that are being counted right now  are legal if you look at a place like  pennsylvania we're not even into the  period of time where we're looking at  the ballots that were  mailed on election day and might have  arrived during the next three days  all of the ballots that were being  reported in pennsylvania were received  by election officials out of the hands  of voters  on election day or earlier so that's  what we're seeing out of pennsylvania  every legal vote  is being counted and we're seeing that  regardless of whether it's in  pennsylvania where there's a democratic  secretary of state  or in georgia or nevada where there are  very good republican secretaries of  state this is a bipartisan  success story about election  administration most of the lawsuits from  the trump campaign  are pretty small potatoes they do not  involve a large number  of votes the number of votes that could  actually swing something however in  nevada there is a more significant claim  of  improper voting from people who do not  actually reside  in the state what kind of evidence  because again we have seen  none so far no one has presented  anything what kind of evidence would be  required  to make that case in a court of law well  first of all we have seen no evidence of  this and something that's very important  to remember is they're basing this based  on some  analysis they claim of the voter files  of the voter list those voter lists are  public  they have been public forever in nevada  they could have had these  months ago they could have done this  analysis months ago they could have  tried to get voters invalidated at any  time during this year or last year or  any of the previous four years  and they did not do that the only reason  they're bringing it now is because  they're losing in nevada  there still remains no evidence of any  improprieties in nevada and again i want  to stress this  a very good republican secretary of  state barbara sagaski  who is running elections in nevada there  was another comment that the president  made  last night at the white house that i  want to get your reaction to it was  regarding  what he termed interference from big  media big money and big  tech interference is a legal sounding  term is there some sort of case that  could be built by a good lawyer  alleging interference in this election  well  i can't imagine there is but remember  also the one election where we have  extensive evidence of interference  by foreign adversaries and others is the  2016 election  which president trump won and where we  know and republicans and democrats have  agreed and his  own department of homeland security and  intelligence community have agreed  that the russians interfered on behalf  of president trump in that election  and we're seeing still some  disinformation campaigns  in this election however there's no  evidence that they were there to  uh to to hurt president trump and in  fact  had any effect on the election in terms  of how people voted  david quickly there are still a lot of  raw memories about the decision in 2000  by the supreme court  court halting a recount in florida  effectively handing the election  to president bush how likely is it this  time around that the supreme court could  intervene in some way that is decisive  well the president seems to think this  is a lot more like 2000 but it's  actually a lot more like 2016.  in 2000 what we saw is extremely  competent teams of lawyers go down to  florida and develop  facts over a long period of time and  then develop legal claims based on those  facts in one state that delivered the  presidency which  in which there was a margin of only 537  votes this is a lot different we're  seeing the president making  some unsubstantiated claims excuse me  all over the country in multiple states  where there's multiple  where the margins are much much greater  yeah if there is evidence we would want  to see it but we have not  seen any yet david becker thank you very  much

